kwalletd6: emit walletAsyncOpened also when the open failed

openAsync() hands back a transaction id and promises the result through the
walletAsyncOpened signal. When openInternal() fails (the user cancelled the
dialog, the collection could not be created or unlocked, ...) we returned
without emitting anything.

On the client side KWallet::Wallet turns that signal into walletOpened(bool),
and that is the only thing an asynchronous caller gets. With no signal the
caller waits forever: no error, no retry, nothing on screen.

libkgapi opens the wallet this way before it starts the Google sign-in, so a
failed open shows up as "the browser never opens" when adding a Gmail account
in KMail. Pressing Previous and Next in the account wizard appears to fix it
only because that creates a second resource which opens the wallet again.

The old kwalletd always emitted the signal, with -1 as the handle on failure,
and KWallet::Wallet already handles that (handle > 0 decides success). Do the
same here.

--- a/src/runtime/kwalletd/kwalletd.cpp
+++ b/src/runtime/kwalletd/kwalletd.cpp
@@ -349,9 +349,10 @@
 
     QTimer::singleShot(0, [this, wallet, wId, appId, tid]() {
         int rnd = openInternal(wallet, wId, appId);
-        if (rnd < 0) {
-            return;
-        }
+        // Report failures as well. KWallet::Wallet only emits walletOpened()
+        // once this signal comes in, so if we keep quiet when the open failed
+        // or the user cancelled the dialog, the application is left waiting
+        // forever. The old kwalletd always answered, with -1 as the handle.
         Q_EMIT walletAsyncOpened(tid, rnd);
     });
 
